#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151874 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "287a493f-e988-5cef-b0d0-4205d1eaf74e" :question/math-academy-id "e-6313" :question/problem "Find a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int \\limits_{C} (y^2-x) \\, \\textrm ds,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r = \\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\le \\theta \\le\\dfrac{ \\pi}4.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151875 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/id #uuid "63aa09fb-9839-5f73-9f98-2eb9784ead43" :question/math-academy-id "e-6314" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac\\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151876 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "00b29cf2-6a73-5fd6-b8bd-04ba8c5f1a46" :question/math-academy-id "e-6315" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} xy(1-x^2) \\,\\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the polar curve $r=\\cos{\\theta}\\:$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83548288-4f27-58cc-ba6e-059820fd4415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385165} {:answer/id #uuid "16cb19a8-67a8-5dfb-917d-91d84b81a316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385166} {:answer/id #uuid "54d78a00-323c-5f2f-a57e-0ca39e12321b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385167} {:answer/id #uuid "a53f75a0-394d-54eb-b5c8-9b0d454f6b21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta -1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385168} {:answer/id #uuid "38823074-d7e2-5f87-b2aa-b870b9b1fea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83548288-4f27-58cc-ba6e-059820fd4415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385165} :answer-field/id #uuid "87608cc6-9b16-56f5-8286-3b55ce88ceac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98c2999a-bf14-50b8-af6e-16c59174bc6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-165413" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$, where $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )=\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6954ef8-68eb-5d87-bae0-04c4d5e9a364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385172} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0325e3-65c2-5662-827f-a99c42fd78a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385173} {:answer/id #uuid "16037d28-9402-560b-ba1c-1ef3992fc5b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385174} {:answer/id #uuid "18dec946-3edd-584a-868f-1b74ec801b5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385175} {:answer/id #uuid "58c7c5d2-a998-5c82-919f-c593ae55c75c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e0325e3-65c2-5662-827f-a99c42fd78a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385173} :answer-field/id #uuid "274cec79-6e16-5ecf-b8ff-5cf3ca30dbe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4c6d207-e6e2-502e-b09e-4d614cf120e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-165414" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le -\\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta }{{(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )=-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta },\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta })}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{y}^{4}}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{-\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6b802fa-d788-59f1-b76a-819af8a17558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150007} {:answer/id #uuid "c28214c3-8b3c-5fe8-976a-e39d16b950cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150008} {:answer/id #uuid "e119b31e-7c2a-50d7-bd3a-623983958a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150009} {:answer/id #uuid "e99ae455-f0b2-52a1-8186-86e4eca9cc4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150010} {:answer/id #uuid "712e3761-3923-55c5-b584-a8d98acf247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "712e3761-3923-55c5-b584-a8d98acf247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150011} :answer-field/id #uuid "872863e2-3052-56ee-bce6-3421ecfba5f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e836460c-d649-52c9-8ad9-0a85310c1949" :question/math-academy-id "q-41188" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aab4a5fd-cfc5-58df-92d8-aec15e4d2809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150877} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6ed9db-99f8-52ae-81b5-c5a5d436e229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{0}^{π}\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150878} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4c1022-42b4-5792-a22a-f9bbfb3bffe2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150879} {:answer/id #uuid "7c787b58-4187-5b65-8fb3-c644d72ea171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150880} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8520f8-9f38-54e7-aec2-0ad39c628b23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{π}\\sin θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aab4a5fd-cfc5-58df-92d8-aec15e4d2809" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150877} :answer-field/id #uuid "30a876f0-c14b-5ea6-be63-7b076908dc79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aefe264e-7dab-56be-82be-12f2dc01af9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76345" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86a7d9dd-d626-507e-b787-091b9f8e253c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(e^{2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150883} {:answer/id #uuid "9be506db-1e12-5261-b7c6-694430a58478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(e - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150884} {:answer/id #uuid "e01eba0a-4402-56b8-ae73-f1cbc0b462a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e^{π/2} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150885} {:answer/id #uuid "15949eb5-dbd3-5184-9f31-2d3810d33c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e^{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150886} {:answer/id #uuid "68a66aaf-e198-5aff-93b6-da13090c52b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{π/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15949eb5-dbd3-5184-9f31-2d3810d33c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(e^{3} - 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150886} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce57c0ff-9f96-5467-ae19-ebb868702961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55dd4f9a-c59d-565d-9412-8e04ab857f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-76347" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the polar curve $r=3$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(3)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot 3\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{e}^{u}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =3({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed82c56f-e330-5ca4-8b49-1dcb4b9de09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150895} {:answer/id #uuid "2032b8fb-1b5b-5b74-bcea-d98fa0390bfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150896} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad46fdb-c96f-5157-8892-c4a6d6621f18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150897} {:answer/id #uuid "7305f421-5cae-5a64-b026-17046998fdce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin^{2} θ\\cos θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150898} {:answer/id #uuid "7b42e790-2c33-55a6-ad34-50392b9ee23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b42e790-2c33-55a6-ad34-50392b9ee23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4∫_{- π/4}^{π/4}\\sin θ\\cos^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150899} :answer-field/id #uuid "b871ac13-c84c-5e91-887e-b02d400b729e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fda4f6af-c917-5961-9062-323f1cdf3aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-76355" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e68ae67-2175-55a3-ac4a-117a340a32a4" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve to a Definite Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f29971-07f7-56a4-9010-85813c75a38f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385179} {:answer/id #uuid "b339bcc7-637d-56b1-985e-39c61548d29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385180} {:answer/id #uuid "221e44a2-1fb4-5a9b-8ead-320bd1d99a02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385181} {:answer/id #uuid "9be00da3-9e6b-576c-9d3e-dead703d056f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (3-{cos}^{6}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385182} {:answer/id #uuid "a4325bbe-088d-5c31-8d83-1c830c118786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b339bcc7-637d-56b1-985e-39c61548d29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385180} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f23dced-8232-5933-a039-1c165614431a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "195fd061-ac6f-58ce-8349-2d0d29798013" :question/math-academy-id "q-76610" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-2{({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (1-3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a25ed59f-4300-5486-b1e7-34c84afef52a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385186} {:answer/id #uuid "24c5d90d-fbfa-5154-9e06-c73345d1a479" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385187} {:answer/id #uuid "60300963-664c-5382-afbc-6f450393d46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385188} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d8f03-7e83-57a8-bc90-9a6dc7f8cf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385189} {:answer/id #uuid "54203cba-d37b-577c-b213-f4acf295a287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe3d8f03-7e83-57a8-bc90-9a6dc7f8cf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385189} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a1b1bc-c9eb-5f8d-b682-6dd0d80e1c10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb5fb2c5-e8b6-5986-a524-7e32fbe5a99c" :question/math-academy-id "q-76616" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ along the polar curve $r=2\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi /2)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425b49cf-a615-5498-99ac-20faee896817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385193} {:answer/id #uuid "820eacea-9a57-570d-8732-dc8043b02ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385194} {:answer/id #uuid "defde5f8-2bd2-5491-bd55-af5957bb24cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385195} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed659d0-41fb-50be-8bf6-04281fbdec80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385196} {:answer/id #uuid "69611954-f2a3-5281-95a8-74d2a1cc8a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69611954-f2a3-5281-95a8-74d2a1cc8a28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385197} :answer-field/id #uuid "f727f2f9-db5f-56d4-bf19-b998820dc50d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac328bb2-ba5c-557e-acf4-f9d1c4e50bed" :question/math-academy-id "q-76658" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}(2y+3x)\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }$, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=2y+3x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2x+3y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(0+3)-(-2+0) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c20ab99-9cc7-5231-80e0-df1ff0f2c01b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385200} {:answer/id #uuid "71af4734-f16a-56f2-84d5-d9d06cf7d4a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385201} {:answer/id #uuid "4d9fa15f-de85-599d-ad95-9427b5358752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385202} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2deff5-64c7-5864-b63a-4c1dc152513b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385203} {:answer/id #uuid "0c1fbabb-b69e-5728-a4b3-b34d9e37e790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c1fbabb-b69e-5728-a4b3-b34d9e37e790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385204} :answer-field/id #uuid "514b2255-c1be-5e2a-99fd-c2a634ba7769" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f601e641-4266-5690-b473-cb0052fef8df" :question/math-academy-id "q-76688" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9964be68-9e22-5d98-b0f8-ae19b6e94c1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Given in Polar Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdae957c-82dd-543c-a27a-d2967f4b608e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385207} {:answer/id #uuid "5e992b30-d25f-5b12-92e9-890700b702b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385208} {:answer/id #uuid "fe969c03-9ca7-5e0e-bf1a-364262b5bac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385209} {:answer/id #uuid "0990785a-2709-5acf-8b45-c5d233e9186b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385210} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8571f7-432e-59af-ae11-a0434030d006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8571f7-432e-59af-ae11-a0434030d006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385211} :answer-field/id #uuid "d32f1e28-049d-5c31-82f6-860dd7e83393" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18c2b400-4bbd-58c5-9ce2-5b0cb277fa26" :question/math-academy-id "q-76690" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta | \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta |=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ because $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta >0.$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2834b1-442e-52a8-8e4b-51608d521082" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385214} {:answer/id #uuid "43523c8c-d8d0-5f3b-82aa-068781f5ae95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385215} {:answer/id #uuid "7aea7361-0272-554f-8e20-d8badc900236" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385216} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0b2d3c-bf96-5149-b850-0c5a306af4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385217} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3e0e2e-88eb-5ff7-98dc-ca1e4e5c970a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43523c8c-d8d0-5f3b-82aa-068781f5ae95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385215} :answer-field/id #uuid "361b08b2-3d38-5e2e-8651-0ff23f573ad4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c72641f-00cf-5a5f-8d00-70ca7eb6de94" :question/math-academy-id "q-76694" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$ along the polar curve $r=2$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{3}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\sqrt{{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}4\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}{sin}^{3/2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =8\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{u}^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}{u}^{5/2}{|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5/2}-{0}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{5}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\sqrt{2}}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "babf811c-ee36-5d17-8b5a-0fd799cdbf8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385221} {:answer/id #uuid "475f7692-67a9-5d30-978f-9a7b37a08499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385222} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7a00c5-1209-5dd9-803b-119ea22a91fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385223} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a9c7aa-6518-5889-b600-6fc29bc4270c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385224} {:answer/id #uuid "632f2702-1f6c-5d81-b2ae-d138081eccb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "babf811c-ee36-5d17-8b5a-0fd799cdbf8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385221} :answer-field/id #uuid "b392ded0-9f1a-5573-96c7-4b02d0a4db59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "336e68e7-a056-5500-aafe-345a8b5f5336" :question/math-academy-id "q-76749" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is given in polar form by $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}8{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =16{\\int }_{1}^{1/2}-{u}^{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-16\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{u}^{6}{|}_{1}^{1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{6}({(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(-\\frac{63}{64}) \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ff45061-98f0-591d-9ec1-00daafaf1015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385228} {:answer/id #uuid "2f9dd01e-4834-58e9-88be-7cc4a2e0f73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385229} {:answer/id #uuid "959882c1-5f3f-5df2-94c6-b96bb9eb195a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385230} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec885f8-f8af-5941-b594-91f1c7c35227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385231} {:answer/id #uuid "703f3bea-6cf1-5d35-981a-712903c7ffcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ff45061-98f0-591d-9ec1-00daafaf1015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385228} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5188d7-0140-5a29-9d15-4b949b1004ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df19f8b4-5f27-5a9b-b009-a17edd72a7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-76765" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the polar curve $r=6$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(6)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}36\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}}u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =3({(3\\sqrt{3})}^{2}-{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee6e8ead-8186-5afe-bd66-d11c55b65dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Line Integral of a Scalar Function Along a Curve Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9336e96-5760-5190-a242-013956841ee2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385235} {:answer/id #uuid "6a064862-924c-5341-8bf9-462e42e43815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385236} {:answer/id #uuid "31e0e66c-2707-5bb0-b011-539c9886f602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385237} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f30664-1b68-59b6-8d26-7844521eb6ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385238} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3cac09-8018-531d-a0b7-c932877c1711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi +1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31e0e66c-2707-5bb0-b011-539c9886f602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385237} :answer-field/id #uuid "a86e6a00-db6e-5152-afef-c42a9f5cfe82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f49db958-1d4b-559b-b1cd-2049c9be9984" :question/math-academy-id "q-76767" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$ along the polar curve $r=1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\theta  & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}-0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{6}+(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi -3}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
